 📣 Situational awareness: Shawn Fain was reelected as president of the UAW by a wide margin, according to the federal monitor in charge of overseeing the union.

 🔔 The dashboard: The S&amp;P 500 closed down essentially flat.

 🥶 Today's stock spotlight: Johnson &amp; Johnson (-5.9%) slipped after reporting weaker than expected medical device sales. 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: Consumers roll on 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 American consumers remain resilient despite higher interest rates and energy costs , according to earnings reports from three of the nation's biggest banks.

 At JPMorgan Chase , credit and debit card sales volume was up 10% compared with a year earlier. At Wells Fargo , debit card spending increased 8% year over year, up from 5% growth a year earlier. Credit card spending jumped 12%. At Citigroup , general-purpose card spending jumped 13%. 💳 And consumers are largely keeping up with their bills, despite concerns about mounting debt . JPMorgan's consumer card net charge-off rate declined to 2.91%, while Wells Fargo and Citi also reported improving consumer credit quality.

 🗣️ The big picture: "While much has been made of the AI boom, the consumer is still driving much of the growth," Citi CEO Jane Fraser said on the bank's earnings call. "The labor market is stable and companies remain active."

 Strong employment and wage growth have helped Americans absorb higher prices, according to Wells Fargo CFO Mike Santomassimo. ⚠️ Yes, but: Bank executives warned that persistently high interest rates could eventually take a toll on consumers, particularly if the labor market weakens.

 The housing market is already feeling the squeeze. Mortgage rates recently climbed above 7%, threatening to further chill an already sluggish market . The bottom line: Consumer sentiment may be at a 12-year low , but Americans are still spending — and, for now, keeping up with their bills.

