 2. BofA hit with $1.2B fine 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo: David Paul Morris/Bloomberg via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Bank of America was hit with more than $1.2 billion in penalties from the Treasury Department for failing to report suspicious transactions and fix longstanding deficiencies in its anti-money-laundering program, Axios' Courtenay Brown writes .

 💸 Why it matters: The action includes the third-largest penalty in Treasury history, following years of compliance failures that persisted even after regulators ordered the bank to strengthen its safeguards.

 The Office of the Comptroller of the Currency also assessed a $750 million penalty, the largest in the agency's history. The other side: In a statement, Bank of America said it has invested billions of dollars in technology and dedicated thousands of employees to fighting financial crime.

 "Our team is far along with and committed to completing the work required to address the issues raised by our regulators." Zoom out: The action comes less than a week after federal regulators fined American Express $350 million over similar failures to detect and report suspected money laundering.

 BofA reports earnings tomorrow.

